<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#iwan">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iwan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as four-iwan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An iwan is a vaulted hall or space, walled on three sides and open on one, characteristic of Persian and Islamic architecture. The four-iwan plan, with iwans facing a central courtyard, became standard for mosques and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>. Its spread is credited as an architectural legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iwan.jpg" alt="Iwan" width="320" height="356" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sven Dirks, Wien · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herat_Masjidi_Jami_iwan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>An iwan is a rectangular hall or space, usually vaulted, walled on three sides, with one end entirely open. Its formal gateway is called a pishtaq, a portal projecting from the facade and usually decorated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a> bands, glazed tilework and geometric designs. The definition allows some interpretation, so scale, material and decoration vary greatly. The form is not confined to any one function and appears in secular and religious buildings, public and residential. The New Persian word eyvān is borrowed from Persian into Arabic and Turkish, and its etymology is unclear. Scholars once derived it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> apadāna, a word <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> used for a structure at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a>, but this is no longer taken for granted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The iwan is pre-Islamic and Iranian in origin. Many scholars discuss its invention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, and there is a general consensus that it evolved locally rather than being imported. What made it a landmark development was the incorporation of a vaulted ceiling, though vaults already existed in Elamite Susa, Assyrian Nineveh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the Mycenaeans. Most scholars now credit the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> with its invention. One of the earliest Parthian iwans stood at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, where the shift from post-and-lintel construction to vaulting occurred around the 1st century CE, and others have been found at Ashur, Hatra, Dura Europos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> also favoured the form but changed its function. A Parthian iwan was a room in itself, while a Sasanian one served as a grand entranceway to a larger, usually domed space. The chief Sasanian example is the Taq Kasra at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, begun under Khosrau I after a campaign against the Eastern Romans in 540. Its hall was about 37 m high, 26 m across and 50 m long, the largest vault built to that time. It was later largely demolished by al-Mansur, who reused its bricks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Islamic architecture borrowed the iwan from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a>. Before the 12th century it appeared mostly in houses, civic structures and palaces, and from the 12th century it became common in religious buildings. It was especially important in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and Greater Iran, and was adopted elsewhere, as at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal</a> and other Mughal mausoleums.</p><p>The four-iwan plan places four iwans around a central courtyard, one often facing the qibla with a mihrab. It was already used in Parthian and Sasanian palaces and temples. Its earliest known Islamic appearance is at the governor's palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, as rebuilt in the late 7th century. It became common in mosques only in the 12th century, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> used it, probably first in their changes to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jameh-mosque-of-isfahan">Great Mosque of Isfahan</a>. It then spread with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a>, and was prominent in the Ayyubid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> madrasas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iwan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tajlu Khanum, Ismail I’s queen, financed the Golden <b>Iwan</b> of the Fatima Masumeh Shrine at Qom in 1519, built her husband’s tomb at Ardabil and supported Tahmasp’s accession in 1524.</blockquote><a href="/societies/safavid-iran">Social Hierarchy of Safavid Iran</a></li></ul></section>
